diff --git a/docs/TESTING_PERFORMANCE.md b/docs/TESTING_PERFORMANCE.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a0eaf7c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/TESTING_PERFORMANCE.md
@@ -0,0 +1,129 @@
+# RSpec performance notes
+
+These measurements cover `spec/models` and `spec/requests` in separate passes. System and streaming specs remain excluded. The initial setup optimization did not alter factories; the later factory optimization is recorded below. Use the same seed for before/after comparisons and do not run multiple spec directories in one profiling command.
+
+## Model spec baseline (2026-11-23)
+
+Command:
+
+```shell
+bundle exec rspec spec/models --format progress --profile 30 --seed 12533
+```
+
+| Run | Examples | RSpec time | Wall time |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Baseline, globally inline Sidekiq | 1795 | 176.9s | 180.361s |
+| Model specs use fake Sidekiq plus shared setup | 1795 | 99.31s | 102.925s |
+
+Both runs completed with zero failures. The final run is 43.9% faster by RSpec time and 42.9% faster by wall time.
+
+## Findings
+
+- EventProf measured 3,908 inline Sidekiq events taking 63.321s, or 29.33% of the instrumented run. Common model callbacks enqueue webhook and mail-related workers while records are fabricated. Model specs now use Sidekiq's fake mode by default; examples that assert actual mail delivery opt into `sidekiq: :inline`.
+- RSpec Dissect measured 111.636s of setup out of 140.242s under fake Sidekiq mode. The largest avoidable setup costs came from recreating the same records for each example in `AccountStatusesCleanupPolicy`, `TagFeed`, and `Form::Import`. These specs now use `let_it_be`/`before_all` for stable database graphs and `refind: true` for fresh Active Record instances in each example. Data mutations remain inside each example's transaction.
+- The first fake-Sidekiq pass ran in 131.8s but found three examples whose assertions depend on mail delivery executing inline. They are isolated with metadata rather than changing their assertions or the global mode for unrelated specs.
+- At the end of the model-only pass, non-model spec types were still unchanged. A model context hook sets fake mode before `let_it_be` setup because `before_all` runs before per-example hooks; the request-specific mode change is recorded below.
+- Factory definitions were intentionally unchanged during this setup-level pass; the follow-up result is recorded below.
+
+## Reprofiling
+
+Profile repeated setup:
+
+```shell
+RD_PROF=1 RD_PROF_TOP=30 RD_PROF_LET_TOP=5 \
+  bundle exec rspec spec/models --require ./spec/rails_helper --require test_prof \
+  --format progress --seed 12533
+```
+
+Profile SQL and Sidekiq events when investigating job-mode costs:
+
+```shell
+EVENT_PROF='sql.active_record,sidekiq.inline' \
+  EVENT_PROF_EXAMPLES=1 EVENT_PROF_TOP=20 \
+  bundle exec rspec spec/models --require ./spec/rails_helper --require test_prof \
+  --format progress --seed 12533
+```
+
+Instrumented runs have profiling overhead; compare final uninstrumented timings for the speed target. After the shared-setup pass, the largest remaining model groups by RSpec time are `Trends::Statuses`, `Status::ThreadingConcern`, and `MediaAttachment`. Request results are recorded below.
+
+## Request spec baseline and optimization (2026-11-23)
+
+Command:
+
+```shell
+bundle exec rspec spec/requests --format progress --profile 30 --seed 12533
+```
+
+| Run | Examples | RSpec time | Wall time |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Baseline, requests use inline Sidekiq | 1877 | 269.0s | 272s |
+| Fake Sidekiq, shared request setup, warn-level logging | 1877 | 100.9s | 103s |
+
+Both runs completed with zero failures. The request suite is 62.5% faster by RSpec time and 62.1% faster by wall time. Together with the model optimization, the original 445.9s combined RSpec time is now 200.21s (2.23x faster); combined wall time is 452.361s versus 205.925s (2.20x faster).
+
+## Request profiling findings
+
+- EventProf measured 7,592 inline Sidekiq events taking 97.617s, 32.12% of the instrumented run. SQL notifications measured 237,901 events taking 55.342s. Instrumented runs took 303.937s, so these values include profiling overhead and are not used for the speed comparison.
+- Request specs now use Sidekiq's fake mode by default. Examples and scopes that require delivered mail or synchronous feed/notification/work side effects opt into `sidekiq: :inline`. Notification fixture graphs are executed inline once in `before_all` and immediately restore fake mode.
+- RSpec Dissect initially measured 92.369s of setup in a 144.823s run. Repeated fixture matrices and service-created graphs in caching, timelines, ActivityPub, notifications, account feeds, reports, and status activity now use `let_it_be`/`before_all` with `refind: true` where records are mutable.
+- The shared API authentication context now shares its base user and default token per group. A fresh token is still fabricated when a nested example changes scopes or changes the authenticated user, so scope/role coverage remains isolated.
+- Test logging runs at warn level so request specs do not format and write the hundreds of thousands of DEBUG SQL entries. A prior request run rotated more than 100 MB of test logs. Redis teardown uses `flushdb`, equivalent to the previous `KEYS` plus delete-all cleanup but with one command per example.
+- Factory definitions and application runtime behavior were not changed during this setup/configuration phase.
+
+## Reprofiling request specs
+
+Profile repeated setup:
+
+```shell
+RD_PROF=1 RD_PROF_TOP=40 RD_PROF_LET_TOP=8 \
+  bundle exec rspec spec/requests --require ./spec/rails_helper --require test_prof \
+  --format progress --seed 12533
+```
+
+Profile SQL and Sidekiq events:
+
+```shell
+EVENT_PROF='sql.active_record,sidekiq.inline' \
+  EVENT_PROF_EXAMPLES=1 EVENT_PROF_TOP=30 \
+  bundle exec rspec spec/requests --require ./spec/rails_helper --require test_prof \
+  --format progress --seed 12533
+```
+
+The remaining slow request groups after this pass are intentionally behavior-heavy: signature verification, large ActivityPub pagination fixtures, and media processing. Keep future comparisons uninstrumented and scoped to `spec/requests`; system and streaming suites remain excluded.
+
+## Factory usage optimization (2026-11-23)
+
+The setup-level optimizations above left the most expensive fixture behavior inside the two highest-volume fabricators: `:status` created a bookmark after every save, and `:user` created two login activities after every save. Neither relationship represented behavior shared by the object being fabricated. Specs that actually exercise bookmarks or login activities create those records explicitly.
+
+FactoryProf was run with the same seed immediately before and after removing those callbacks:
+
+```shell
+FPROF=1 bundle exec rspec spec/models --require test_prof --format progress --seed 12533
+FPROF=1 bundle exec rspec spec/requests --require test_prof --format progress --seed 12533
+```
+
+| Group | Run | Examples | Factory calls | Bookmark calls | Login activity calls | Factory top-level time | RSpec time |
+| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
+| Models | Before | 1795 | 3877 | 717 | 270 | 50.500s | 61.57s |
+| Models | After | 1795 | 2894 | 4 | 0 | 39.786s | 51.36s |
+| Requests | Before | 1877 | 4879 | 436 | 1254 | 47.483s | 104.58s |
+| Requests | After | 1877 | 3198 | 9 | 0 | 32.861s | 78.63s |
+
+The callback removal eliminated 2,664 unrelated records. Model RSpec time dropped 16.6%, request RSpec time dropped 24.9%, and the instrumented combined result dropped from 166.15s to 129.99s (21.8%). Top-level factory time measures the elapsed time inside top-level fabrication calls, so nested callbacks are the main cost represented here; it is not equivalent to whole-suite time.
+
+Final uninstrumented runs with the established command shape completed with zero failures:
+
+```shell
+bundle exec rspec spec/models --format progress --profile 30 --seed 12533
+bundle exec rspec spec/requests --format progress --profile 30 --seed 12533
+```
+
+| Group | Examples | RSpec time |
+| --- | ---: | ---: |
+| Models | 1795 | 37.56s |
+| Requests | 1877 | 64.37s |
+| Combined | 3672 | 101.93s |
+
+The combined result is 4.37 times faster than the original 445.9s RSpec-time baseline. Use the uninstrumented commands for the speed target; FactoryProf itself adds substantial overhead but is useful for controlled factory comparisons.
+
+The remaining factory hotspots are media attachment processing (5.19s in the model profile and 2.04s in the request profile) and the account/user graph. Do not reintroduce after-create records that most examples never request. Create bookmarks and login activities explicitly in the fixtures or contexts that assert their behavior; that keeps factory state representative and makes costly fixture intent visible.
